===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable}} An [[abominable]] act; a [[disgusting]] [[vice]]; a [[despicable]] [[habit]]. {{defdate|from ca. 1150–1350}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=6}}</ref>
#: {{syn|en|perversion}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Antony and Cleopatra|III|vi|354|columns=1–2||Onely th’adulterous ''Anthony'', most large / In his '''abhominations''', turnes you off}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1936|author=Rollo Ahmed|title=The Black Art|publisher=Long|location=London|page=160|passage=Religious sodomy was practised by male prostitutes in the Hebrew temple groves, which was one of the '''abominations''' of Israel that Josiah cleared away.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The feeling of extreme [[disgust]] and hatred {{defdate|from ca. 1350–1470}}<ref name=SOED/>
#: {{syn|en|abhorrence|aversion|detestation|disgust|loathing|loathsomeness|odiousness}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|uncountable}} A state that excites detestation or abhorrence; pollution. {{defdate|ca. 1350–1470 to late 15th c.}}<ref name=SOED/>
# {{lb|en|countable}} That which is [[abominable]], [[shameful]]ly vile; an object that excites disgust and hatred {{q|often with religious undertones}}. {{defdate|from ca. 1350–1470}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Kathleen Jenks|entry=Cronus|editor=Brian Kinsey|title=Gods and Goddesses of Greece and Rome|isbn=9780761499800|page=61|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=kBlfEAAAQBAJ&pg=PA61|text=Appalled by a child she found hideous, Philyra begged the gods to free her from having to rear such an '''abomination'''.}}

